  "image_name": "1a74780d2d3b65a57f3df70b27d8e8db.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed pathway linking CAR T-cell therapy at a tumour site to inflammatory effects in the central nervous system. It is organized across three connected compartments: tumour tissue, blood, and CNS, separated by the blood vessel endothelium and the blood–brain barrier.\n\nAt the tumour site, CAR T cells interact with tumour cells and appear to trigger inflammatory cytokines and chemokines. These signals are associated with recruitment of additional CAR T cells and with monocyte recruitment and macrophage activation. In the blood, infused CAR T cells and circulating monocytes are shown as intermediates carrying or amplifying this inflammatory response.\n\nOn the CNS side, the same inflammatory mediators appear to cross or act across the blood–brain barrier, while CAR T cells are recruited into the CNS. The diagram also highlights possible monocyte recruitment and macrophage activation in the CNS, marked with some uncertainty, and a disruption of the BBB. These events lead to activation of resident microglia, suggesting a neuroinflammatory cascade.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that systemic and tumour-derived immune activation during CAR T therapy can propagate through blood-borne cells and cytokines to the brain, potentially causing CNS toxicity.",
